Introduction
A daily loss limit has two parts. One is the amount. The other is the moment at which the counting starts again. The amount is easy to put into an input. The moment is harder, because the rules that name it do so in a time zone. The
Trading Objectives page of FTMO
, for example, says that the daily loss limit is recalculated at 00:00 CET (CEST in summer) from the balance recorded at that time. Other firms define different reset times. I did not verify those times. What matters is that the reset is defined in a specific time zone. That zone may differ from the zone of the broker's server.
An EA normally takes the day from the server clock. Such an EA reads TimeCurrent(), sets the hours, minutes, and seconds to zero, and calls the result today. Article
23732
does this in a daily loss circuit breaker. The article gives a reason: the server stamps every deal, so the server midnight stays consistent with the broker's own records. This rationale is sound for a limit that resets at server midnight. However, it does not cover a limit that resets at 17:00 in New York or at midnight in Prague. Prague uses CET and CEST, the zone of the FTMO reset.
On the server I tested, the three obvious choices give three different day starts. Server midnight is 00:00 on the server clock. Midnight in Central Europe is 01:00 on the same clock all year. The New York close at 17:00 is 00:00 on most days and 23:00 in a few weeks of each year. The United States and Europe change their clocks on different Sundays. A daily figure computed with the wrong one of these moves deals from one day to the next.
In this article, the New York close always means the clock time 17:00 in New York. The moment at which the broker's feed stops before the weekend is a different thing, and I call it the end of the feed. The section on fitting the server clock shows a week in which the two differ by one hour.
This article builds a small set of include files around one question. Given a server time, to which trading day does that time belong, and when does the day start and end? The question takes three inputs: the zone of the server clock, the zone in which the reset is defined, and the reset time. The files consist of the following parts.
CTradingDay resolves a server time to the start, the end, and an ID of its trading day.
CServerClockFit finds the zone of the server clock from the hourly bars of a forex symbol.
CDayTracker notices when a new day starts and keeps the balance and equity of that moment. The tracker can also rebuild the balance of the day start from the deal history.
CDealDays groups the deals of the account history into the trading days of a chosen definition.
I tested the code in four ways. There are 47 unit checks, and 13 of these checks compare the date arithmetic with the IANA time zone database. Three Strategy Tester runs on MetaQuotes-Demo track three definitions side by side. A Python consistency check refits the server zone and rebuilds the day boundaries from the same database. A mutation test had to detect two wrong rules. In the runs, all logged day starts and ends matched the database clock times for the fitted server zone, New York, and Prague. The balance rebuilt from the history matched the balance noted at the day start on every finished day except the first day of each run. The CDayTracker section explains why the first day is excluded. Those statements hold for one server, one build, and the definitions listed in the Strategy Tester section.
The Python check takes the fitted zone as its input, so it does not show that the fit is right. In this article, the only check of the zone that does not come from the bars is one live reading of the server clock. That reading agrees with the fitted offset on the day it was taken.
One result of the fit matters for the New York close. The server I used follows the European rule, UTC+2 with the change on the last Sunday of March and October. In 17 of 248 weekly samples the European and the US rule predict different offsets, and in 16 of those weeks the measured offset followed the European rule. In the weeks between the US and the European change, 17:00 in New York is 23:00 on the server clock, not midnight.
What is already published
Three published articles come close to this topic. The two parts of Dealing with Time,
part 1
and
part 2
, derive the time shifts of a broker from the last Friday hour of EURUSD in hourly bars, and from there the time in New York. Both parts use macros and global variables, and they note that the clock functions are of no help in the Strategy Tester. Article
16171
detects the broker's time zone and daylight saving time (DST) rule from the volatility of non-farm payroll releases in the economic calendar. Article
23732
builds a daily loss circuit breaker that resets at server midnight.
The MQL5 reference agrees with these articles on the clocks. For TimeGMT() it says that, in the Strategy Tester, the function is always equal to the simulated server time of TimeTradeServer(). The Strategy Tester section prints all the clock functions in the tester to confirm this for the build I used.
What I add differs in four points. The day definition is an object with a reset zone and a reset time as inputs. A reset time that falls into a skipped or repeated hour has a defined and tested result. The zone fit uses the last hourly bar of every week and scores every candidate zone, so a result that cannot be told apart from another one is reported as such. And the day boundaries, the zone rules, and the deal grouping are compared with the IANA database through a second implementation in Python, for the server zone that the fit found. I searched the MQL5 article index with several phrases and found no article that treats the reset time as an input or covers skipped and repeated reset times. A search like that does not prove that none exists.
Architecture
The core module consists of six include files. The archive also holds a seventh include file with test vectors, three scripts, one Expert Advisor, and two Python scripts. Fig. 1 shows how the parts depend on each other.
Parts of the trading day resolver
From the hourly bars and the account history to the journal and the Python check
Fig. 1. CServerClockFit turns hourly bars into a server zone, CTradingDay uses it to resolve days, CDayTracker and CDealDays build on CTradingDay, and TdCalendar.mqh holds the rules that every class uses
Every class works on three kinds of time. A server time is a reading of the server clock, the value that TimeCurrent() and the history functions use. A UTC time is the same instant without any zone. A local time is a reading of the clock in the reset zone. Every conversion between these three kinds of time goes through the calendar functions of TdCalendar.mqh.
Time zones and the DST calendar
A zone has two properties. One is the offset from UTC on standard time, and the other is a DST rule that adds one hour. I support three rules. TD_DST_NONE never changes the offset. TD_DST_US and TD_DST_EU follow the schedules of the United States and the European Union in the northern hemisphere.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Daylight saving rule of a time zone (northern hemisphere only)   |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
enum
ENUM_TD_DST
  {
   TD_DST_NONE=
0
,
// the offset never changes
TD_DST_US,
// second Sunday of March to first Sunday of November, at 02:00 local time
TD_DST_EU
// last Sunday of March to last Sunday of October, at 01:00 UTC
};
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| A time zone: standard offset from UTC and a daylight saving rule |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
struct
STdZone
  {
int
std_offset;
// seconds to add to UTC on standard time
ENUM_TD_DST       dst;
// daylight saving rule, one hour when active
};
The two rules differ in how they define the moment of the change. The US rule changes at 02:00 local time on the second Sunday of March and the first Sunday of November. The EU rule changes for all member states at 01:00 UTC on the last Sunday of March and October. The DST window for a given year is computed from the n-th Sunday of a month.
