The offset at an instant is then the standard offset, plus one hour inside the window.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| TdOffsetAtUtc                                                    |
//| Offset from UTC, in seconds, that the zone has at an instant.    |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
int
TdOffsetAtUtc(
const
STdZone &zone,
const
datetime
utc)
  {
datetime
start,end;
if
(TdDstWindowUtc(zone,TdYear(utc),start,end) && utc>=start && utc<end)
return
(zone.std_offset+TD_HOUR);
return
(zone.std_offset);
  }
The reverse direction is the difficult one. A local reading can belong to zero, one, or two instants. When the clock jumps forward, the readings in the skipped hour never appear. When it goes back, the readings in the repeated hour appear twice. In New York, a reset time of 02:30 falls into the skipped hour once a year, in March. A reset time of 01:30 falls into the repeated hour once a year, in November. The function therefore has to say what it returns in both cases.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| TdLocalToUtc                                                     |
//| UTC instant for a wall clock reading. A reading that happens     |
//| twice follows the fold argument. A skipped reading is read with  |
//| the offset from before the jump, so it lands one hour later.     |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
datetime
TdLocalToUtc(
const
STdZone &zone,
const
datetime
local,
const
ENUM_TD_FOLD fold,ENUM_TD_LOCAL &kind)
  {
datetime
std_utc=local-zone.std_offset;
datetime
dst_utc=local-(zone.std_offset+TD_HOUR);
bool
std_ok=(TdOffsetAtUtc(zone,std_utc)==zone.std_offset);
bool
dst_ok=(zone.dst!=TD_DST_NONE && TdOffsetAtUtc(zone,dst_utc)==zone.std_offset+TD_HOUR);
if
(std_ok && dst_ok)
     {
      kind=TD_LOCAL_FOLD;
return
(fold==TD_FOLD_FIRST ? dst_utc : std_utc);
     }
if
(std_ok || dst_ok)
     {
      kind=TD_LOCAL_OK;
return
(std_ok ? std_utc : dst_utc);
     }
   kind=TD_LOCAL_GAP;
return
(std_utc);
  }
For a repeated reading, the caller chooses the first or the second instant with the fold argument. For a skipped reading, the function uses the offset from before the jump, so the boundary lands one hour later on the clock. The outcome is reported in the kind argument. Fig. 2 shows both cases for New York, with the values that the unit tests use.
A reset time inside a skipped hour and inside a repeated hour
New York, the two clock changes of one year, drawn on a UTC axis
Fig. 2. A reset time of 02:30 that the New York clock skips resolves to 07:30 UTC, and a reset time of 01:30 that the clock shows twice resolves to 05:30 UTC for the first reading and 06:30 UTC for the second
A skipped reading always moves forward in my version, regardless of the fold argument. Python's zoneinfo moves a skipped reading back by one hour when fold is 1. I did not copy that behavior, and the unit tests follow this choice: the vectors for skipped readings use fold 0 only.
