The ID of a day is the number of days since 1970.01.01 of the local date on which the day starts, in the reset zone. Consecutive days differ by one. Two server times that the server clock shows only once have the same ID exactly when they belong to the same day.
A server clock with DST shows one hour twice each year. A server time alone cannot say which of the two instants is meant. The class reads such a time as the first instant. On the test server that hour runs from 03:00 to 04:00 on a Sunday in October, when the market is closed. For a definition whose boundary falls into that hour, CDayTracker reports the new day at the end of the hour. The unit tests cover each of these rules.
A day that has no deals or ticks, such as a Saturday, still has an ID. A jump of more than one between the IDs of two logged days therefore means that no call came in the days between. Such a jump happens on a weekend or a holiday, and also while the EA is not running.
The ID is the date on which the day starts. A New York close day that starts on Thursday at 17:00 therefore has the ID of the Thursday, although most of its hours fall on the Friday. An EA that names the day after its end date has to add one.
Finding the server time zone from Friday closes
CTradingDay needs the zone of the server clock, and a broker rarely states it. The fit class finds it from data. The idea is the one used in the Dealing with Time articles. I assume that the forex week ends at 17:00 in New York on Friday, as those articles do. The hour bar that ends at that moment is then the last bar of the week. Its server time minus the UTC time of 17:00 in New York is the server offset in that week.
I first planned to use the Sunday reopening as well. The script prints how many bars open on a Sunday and how many weekend gaps end with a bar at Monday 00:00. In the MetaQuotes-Demo data, no bar opens on a Sunday, and 244 of the 250 gaps end at Monday 00:00. Only the Friday close is usable there. The class looks for a gap of more than 24 hours that follows a Friday. It converts 17:00 New York on that Friday to UTC and keeps the difference to the bar time. The difference counts only if it is a whole number of hours within 14 hours of UTC. Any other gap is counted as skipped.
