The class then scores every candidate zone by the number of weeks in which the zone predicts the measured offset. The candidates run from -12 to +14 hours, each with no rule, the US rule, or the EU rule. The fit keeps the candidate with the highest score. A tie goes to the candidate that the loop tries first. The loop runs from the lowest offset to the highest, and for each offset it tries no rule, the US rule, and then the EU rule. The score of the second best is kept as well, because an equal score means that the history cannot tell the two zones apart. The caller sees a tie as BestHits() equal to RunnerHits(), and the text of Summary() contains both scores. A history that covers only winter weeks, for example, fits all three rules equally well.
