The fit chose UTC+2 with the EU rule. That zone explains 246 of 248 weeks, and the US rule with the same base explains 231. The Python check, described below, counts 17 weeks in which the two rules predict different offsets. The EU rule is right in 16 of those weeks and the US rule in one. Fig. 3 shows the weekly residuals of both rules.
The live reading gives +3 hours, and the tick time lay 22 seconds behind the computer clock. The fitted zone gives the same offset for that day, because Europe was still on DST. A zone of UTC+3 without DST would give the same reading. The fit tells those two zones apart through the winter weeks, and the live reading alone does not.
