Three details matter for a daily limit. The start values come from the first call of the day, not from the exact boundary second, so a day after a weekend starts with the first tick of Monday. A call with a time earlier than the current day is ignored. And a call that comes after a gap of several days reports one new day, not several.
The first detail is a significant limitation for a rule that measures the loss from the equity at the reset moment. The tracker has no equity value for that moment. After a weekend or a quiet hour, the equity of the first call already includes the price change of the open positions since the reset. The start balance is less exposed, because a trade needs a tick. A deposit or a withdrawal between the reset and the first call would still end up in the start balance.
The balance at the start of a day can also be rebuilt from the history. The balance at the end of the window minus the net result of every deal inside the window is the balance at its start. Deposits and withdrawals are balance deals, so they are included. A deposit inside the window counts as part of the change, and the rebuilt balance is the balance before it. Credit deals are skipped, because a credit changes the credit of the account and not its balance. The only balance deal in my runs is the initial deposit of the tester, and the paragraph after the code shows how it appeared. I had no account with credit deals, so the credit branch is not tested.
Every window in the module covers its start and not its end. The convention holds for a trading day, for DealsNet(), for CDealDays, and for the Python check. A deal at the exact second of a boundary therefore belongs to the day that starts there. My first version of DealsNet() included both ends, and the demo passed the day end minus one second to make up for it. The function now applies the same convention as the day, and the demo passes the start and the end of a day as they are.
