Not everything is compared with the database. A scan of three years at 7-minute steps tests properties of the resolver against itself. Every instant lies inside its own day, and the day is between 23 and 25 hours long. The day of a day start is that same day, and the ID never goes down. These checks show that the logic is consistent. They do not show that the logic is right. That part rests on the comparison with the database above.
The next four checks keep the reset zone fixed and let only the server clock change. With a reset at midnight UTC on a server with the EU rule, every day of three years must be 24 hours long. With a reset at 01:00 UTC, the day that ends on the last Sunday of October 2025 ends at 03:00 on the server clock. The server shows 03:00 twice on that night, and the boundary is the second reading. The checks expect a length of 24 hours for that day, a server time of 03:30 read as its first instant, and a tracker that reports the new day at 04:00.
The fit checks build synthetic weeks of hourly bars for a server with a known zone and let the class find it. They cover a server with the EU rule, one with the US rule, and one without a rule. They also cover three early-close weeks, a history of winter weeks only, and a history that is too short. The early-close weeks have to stay unexplained, and the winter history has to end in a tie. The tracker checks follow the three details listed in the CDayTracker section, and the last checks test that Init() refuses reset times and offsets outside their range.
